age, and C's 185 failures are therefore measurements of this hand-made set, not something that would necessarily survive a different 72 protocols.","falsifier":"Search the supplied abstract and three candidate sections for a sampling frame, holdout, or alternative corpus — none is present.","fix":"State each headline figure as corpus-bound and add category sizes plus one sensitivity (drop two protocols or one category).","status":"verified"},{"id":"F3","candidate":"B","severity":"high","claim":"Across 60 constructions, 15 are inadmissible. A and C report that 61 of 72 protocols satisfy the laws/requirements and warrants (11 fail).","problem":"The same campaign reports 15/60 inadmissible constructions and 11/72 protocols failing requirements and warrants without a join. Readers cannot tell whether the predicates differ, whether the 60-slice is a different population, or whether a figure has drifted.","falsifier":"Cross-tab the 72 protocols by {in the 60 constructions, satisfies requirements and warrants, inadmissible} from B's '15 constructions are inadmissible' against A's and C's '61 of 72'.","fix":"Publish one denominator table that defines construction vs protocol and inadmissible vs fails-requirements-and-warrants.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F4","candidate":"C","severity":"medium","claim":"Every failure arms a prohibition; requirements, warrants and grounding contribute none. Attribution of the failures resolves to 15 arcs and 10 elements with a strict below-set.","problem":"The abstract already states that sets satisfying requirements form a complete lattice under union, so among the 61, pairwise requirement failure is impossible by construction. Reporting that requirements contribute none is true but not interesting. The 15 arcs and 10 elements are not listed, so the below-set concentration is asserted in the bundle.","falsifier":"Abstract sentence that the sets satisfying the positive constraints form a complete lattice under union; C's paragraph does not name the 15 arcs or 10 elements.","fix":"Call requirement-closure a theorem. Report warrant and grounding attribution as the measurement. List the 15 arcs and 10 elements.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F5","candidate":"A","severity":"medium","claim":"Six of seven perpetuals omit exactly {Ct, Ex, Li}; Hyperliquid 14→11 and GMX v2 13→10; Derive and Aevo exhibit the same signature. Supporting figures: 61 of 72 satisfy the laws and warrants; 29 canonical forms are strictly smaller.","problem":"Reduction counts exist only for two of eight named protocols. n=7 (n=2 for options) cannot carry a category law, and 61/72 plus 29 smaller do not bear on {Ct, Ex, Li}. A practitioner reading those protocols could obtain the omission list.","falsifier":"A's paragraph gives 14→11 and 13→10 only for Hyperliquid and GMX v2; no sizes for ApeX, Aster, Lighter, edgeX, Derive, or Aevo.","fix":"Table canonical-form sizes for all eight. Move 61/72 and 29 smaller out of A's supporting figures unless they are shown to be the same reduction.","status":"verified"},{"id":"F6","candidate":"B","severity":"medium","claim":"Of 1259 functional obligations on 60 constructions, the vocabulary covers 570 (45.3% permissive, 29.0% strict); 689 are residue; 205 rows are approximate fits. A vocabulary whose positive theory excludes nothing fails by having no name.","problem":"570/1259=45.3% and (570-205)/1259=29.0% check out, so the percentages are load-bearing for this labeling. They do not show the 689 failed by having no name rather than by coder granularity: ~21 hand-made obligations per construction, with 205 already in a third bucket, and no per-construction histogram or codebook.","falsifier":"Recompute 570/1259 and (570-205)/1259 from B's counts; search B for a residue-per-construction distribution or coding rule — neither is present.","fix":"Report residue per construction (median and IQR) and the rule that separates approximate from uncovered. Bound the claim to this codebook, not to DeFi.","status"